#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384829 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4636870-89db-5bf9-8a04-777407957e33" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Parallel to Another Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c0aa963-0a03-5c71-a92c-d39d53224e9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,1,-10)$ and $(-1,1,6)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384831} {:answer/id #uuid "a0a862fa-4bce-59e3-8814-cae185814e4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,4,8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384832} {:answer/id #uuid "ba6ef4e7-e098-539e-814c-4ab54f6240c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $(0,4,8)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384833} {:answer/id #uuid "f3017cf7-4fdd-53c0-9a64-f67616761c60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,1,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384834} {:answer/id #uuid "4e647582-3df9-549a-a678-3e528cbee9fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,1,-10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0a862fa-4bce-59e3-8814-cae185814e4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,4,8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384832} :answer-field/id #uuid "75d162fc-fa25-54c2-902f-d78114c9c4d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384830}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "47616907-68ba-5ff3-ae24-1153f4ea3fed" :question/math-academy-id "q-165040" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2}-2t\\,,\\,{t}^{2}\\,,\\,8t-2{t}^{2}⟩,$ find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $⟨1\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8t-2{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t-2,\\,2t,\\,8-4t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c\\,⟨1\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c\\,⟨1\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2t-2,\\,2t,\\,8-4t⟩ & =c\\,⟨1\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2t-2,\\,2t,\\,8-4t⟩ & =⟨c\\,,\\,2c\\,,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2t-2=c \\\\ 2t=2c \\\\ 8-4t=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we obtain that $t=2.$ Substituting to the second equation, we get $c=2.$\nThese values also satisfy the first equation. \n\nTherefore, $⟨1\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0⟩$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=2.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =⟨{2}^{2}-2(2)\\,,\\,{2}^{2}\\,,\\,8(2)-2(2)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,4,\\,8⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨1\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(0,4,8).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384864 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "297a4812-a87a-592a-818a-8c8d3d3f7868" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to Another Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a1cf6b7-c4db-5e2a-ab3b-bdae218f32af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,-7)$ and $(\\frac{7}{9}\\,,\\,-\\frac{37}{27})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384866} {:answer/id #uuid "c276f960-8bb5-5ebf-b5fe-cfaeeab8cbf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{7}{9}\\,,\\,-\\frac{37}{27})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384867} {:answer/id #uuid "c8c001be-d3ba-5db9-88c3-50ac6855ef83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384868} {:answer/id #uuid "d5825a57-dd67-512c-a791-252eebf2d7d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(\\frac{4}{3}\\,,\\,\\frac{16}{9})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384869} {:answer/id #uuid "f451166f-d800-5ebe-be3f-3f43a56f6409" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{4}{3}\\,,\\,\\frac{16}{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384870}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c276f960-8bb5-5ebf-b5fe-cfaeeab8cbf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{7}{9}\\,,\\,-\\frac{37}{27})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384867} :answer-field/id #uuid "57e6bf08-2a63-5c42-a511-7482134d283f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a15bf605-19f6-54de-9516-f3cb67a15bf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-75205" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2}-1\\,,\\,{t}^{3}+1⟩,t\\in (-\\infty ,0)$ find all of the points at which the tangent to the curve is perpendicular to the vector $⟨2\\,,\\,1⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-1)\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t\\,,\\,3{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors  $⟨2\\,,\\,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $⟨2\\,,\\,1⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨2\\,,\\,1⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨2\\,,\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨2t\\,,\\,3{t}^{2}⟩ & =0 \\\\ 2\\cdot 2t+1\\cdot 3{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 4t+3{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ t(4+3t) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\in (-\\infty ,0)$, the only solution is $t=-\\frac{4}{3}$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-\\frac{4}{3}) & =⟨{(-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}-1\\,,\\,{(-\\frac{4}{3})}^{3}+1⟩=⟨\\frac{7}{9}\\,,\\,-\\frac{37}{27}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $⟨2\\,,\\,-1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(\\frac{7}{9}\\,,\\,-\\frac{37}{27}).$"}]}
